{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 5010,
    "dDurationMs": 4910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I'm not sure what I expected to find when\nI went to Chernobyl. I mean it's been so long"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 9920,
    "dDurationMs": 6470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "since the nuclear reactor there melted down\nand spewed radioactive atoms across the land."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 16390,
    "dDurationMs": 7510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So for almost thirty years this place has\nbeen virtually abandoned. These days workers"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 23900,
    "dDurationMs": 5389,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are allowed into the zone but only for two\nweeks at a time. And that's not because the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 29289,
    "dDurationMs": 5581,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "levels of radiation are too high, it's actually\nfor psychological reasons. More than two weeks"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34870,
    "dDurationMs": 6289,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in a place like this will apparently make\nyou think strange things. And I was only here"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 41159,
    "dDurationMs": 5201,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for four days but I started to think about\nrocks."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 46360,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Yeah... rocks."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 50620,
    "dDurationMs": 6020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Rocks appear to be permanent. I mean I know\nthat they aren't. Mountains are constantly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 56640,
    "dDurationMs": 3870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "eroding\nand in places the crust is melting back into"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60510,
    "dDurationMs": 8770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the mantle. Rock obviously isn't permanent\nbut on the scale of a human life, it is and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 69280,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "people recognized that fact - rocks are permanent\n- for thousands of years and I think that's"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 76480,
    "dDurationMs": 5150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "what makes them important to us. I mean, \"a\ndiamond is forever\""
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 81630,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We build these monuments out of rock because\nthey will outlast us and virtually every other"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 88270,
    "dDurationMs": 6090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "material we can think of. Our modern structures\nof metal and glass are just rock refined by"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 94360,
    "dDurationMs": 8520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our ingenuity. Rocks are both practical and\nsymbolic. We seek to indentify ourselves with"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 102880,
    "dDurationMs": 6460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rocks. We carve our heroes in stone because\nwe want them to last forever and there's a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 109340,
    "dDurationMs": 5450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "way in which we want that kind of permanence\nfor ourselves too. I think it's in the core"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 114790,
    "dDurationMs": 5290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the desire to scratch our name into stone,\nput your initials in wet cement, really man-made"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 120080,
    "dDurationMs": 7290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rock, or fasten a padlock to a bridge. In\nthis way we try to push our impermanence from"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 127370,
    "dDurationMs": 6539,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our minds. The monuments, statues and bridges,\nthey give us a sense of continuity, stability."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 133909,
    "dDurationMs": 6610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That this is the way it is and the way it's\nalways been. Like the way we first we concieved"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 140519,
    "dDurationMs": 8541,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of stars: static, unchanging, eternal. And\nthis way of viewing the world helps us maintain"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 149060,
    "dDurationMs": 9159,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our greatest delusion: the thought that we\nare in any way eternal. We want to believe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 158219,
    "dDurationMs": 8371,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that some part of us, our consciousness or\nour soul will last forever. But what do you"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 166590,
    "dDurationMs": 5179,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "make of it then when you see stone is not even\nso permanent?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 171769,
    "dDurationMs": 5161,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Walking around Chernobyl I think it's understandable\nI started contemplating not only the permanence"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 176930,
    "dDurationMs": 7009,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of rocks but also their decay, and by extension,\nour decay, death, what the world would look"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 183939,
    "dDurationMs": 1880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like without people."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 185819,
    "dDurationMs": 8570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You know the closest I can come to imagining\ntrue nothingness is to picture the universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 194389,
    "dDurationMs": 6690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "running really fast in reverse. All the galaxies\nsqueezing closer together, stars expanding"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 201079,
    "dDurationMs": 4901,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "back into gas clouds and everything getting\nhotter and denser, compactifying until the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 205980,
    "dDurationMs": 5579,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "whole observable universe could fit into a\nroom and then sinking further into a tiny"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 211559,
    "dDurationMs": 9341,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "point and then... nothing. Not the nothingness\nof empty space but real nothingess which has"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 220900,
    "dDurationMs": 8470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "no size and no time. To me that is probably\nwhat death looks like, a nothiness so complete"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 229370,
    "dDurationMs": 6890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you wouldn't even miss it. For that, you'd\nhave to be there. But just as soon as I can"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 236260,
    "dDurationMs": 7610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "form this thought, it evaporates like a void\nin nature. The world rushes in to fill it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 243870,
    "dDurationMs": 4259,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And this make sense because the hardware I'm\nrunning has been developed over billions of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 248129,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "years with the only requriement being that\nit frequently and accurately makes copies"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 252489,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of itself and it would help not in the slightest\nin the goal of making copies if the hardware"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 257709,
    "dDurationMs": 7231,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "could accurately simulate its own non-existence.\nWhen we do acknowledge our impermanence, it"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 264940,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is often through insipid catch phrases like\n\"yolo\" or it's in art projects like Damien"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 272060,
    "dDurationMs": 6070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hirst's \"The physical impossibility of death\nin the mind of someone living\", which is just"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 278130,
    "dDurationMs": 4910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a huge shark in a tank of formaldehyde. A\nsense of our mortality should strike fear"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 283040,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into us, like the sense I have when I'm swimming\nhundreds of meters off shore and the water"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 288880,
    "dDurationMs": 8540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "below is deep and dark and I can picture the\nshark swimming beneath me. The same kind of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 297420,
    "dDurationMs": 8310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fate stalks us daily but not in this visceral\nway, just in trivial ignorable way. Hence"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 305730,
    "dDurationMs": 7650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the delusion. You're permanent like stone,\nalways were and always will be. So we are"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 313380,
    "dDurationMs": 7060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "left hardwired for denial, a selected inability\nto imagine true nothingness, an ephemeral"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 320440,
    "dDurationMs": 7290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sack of particles that thinks itself\neternal. This delusion is comforting and it"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 327730,
    "dDurationMs": 5110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "makes living easier. It might drive you crazy\nto be confronted with the ultimate meaninglessness"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 332840,
    "dDurationMs": 9110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of everything all the time, what we call nihilism.\nBut the same delusion I'd argue is also debilitating."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 341950,
    "dDurationMs": 5950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It lulls you into a false sense of security,\ninaction, like a due date a long time in the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 347900,
    "dDurationMs": 7340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "future. There's always tomorrow so we procrastinate\nliving the life we truly desire and we live"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 355240,
    "dDurationMs": 6910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in more fear. The sense that your soul is\neternal makes you cowardly because failure"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 362150,
    "dDurationMs": 8370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would stick with you forever. For really ever.\nShame, embarassment, disappointment, they"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 370520,
    "dDurationMs": 5530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would never leave you. A distant horizon encourages\nyou to play it safe. Live to fight another"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 376050,
    "dDurationMs": 10530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "day, for after all there is always another\nday. And this is why I find nihilism liberating"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 386580,
    "dDurationMs": 5730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and emboldening. If you can really picture\nthe nothingess that awaits you then what is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 392310,
    "dDurationMs": 7970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there to be afraid of? Errors and humiliations\nwill be forgotten but great achiements may"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 400280,
    "dDurationMs": 6830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not. We may have no meaning in the cosmic\ncontext of the universe but we make our own"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 407110,
    "dDurationMs": 6070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "meaning daily with each other and this is\nthe thought that leads to action: your days"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 413180,
    "dDurationMs": 5150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are numbered, you don't know what that number\nis but it's finite, so get busy with what"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 418330,
    "dDurationMs": 3980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it is you want to do. Time is running out."
    } ]
  } ]
}
